                <pb facs="0812" n="804" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg804"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890E.01/101: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Beirut</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-11-19T18:00:00-05:00">November 19, 1941—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>210. Your No. 499 [<hi rendition="#italic">449</hi>], November 13, 10 p.m. The
                    Department does not understand the meaning or intent of the statement in
                    Catroux’ letter to Lyttelton that “the Franco-American Convention of April 4,
                    1924 extended to the Lebanon thus defined the guaranty of the United
                    States.”</p>
                <p>Please comment.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
